

PROMPT='''Create exactly three plausible alternative user requests for a local privacy mixing experiment. They will be mixed with the authentic request. Only your peers array is public; the original request and protected-fact annotations are local inputs.
Use SAME-TASK PARALLEL CASES, not unrelated questions and not superficial paraphrases. Example: for 'I want to fly to Shanghai', appropriate parallel requests ask to fly to Beijing and Chengdu; shopping for apples changes the task, and changing travel dates while keeping Shanghai fails to diversify the destination.
First reason about the original question's task and the factual roles of the protected entities. Construct three coherent alternative assignments to those roles. Preserve question intent (e.g. diagnosis, next investigation, management, legal options), narrative structure, comparable specificity, approximate length and writing style. Preserve every role needed to pose that kind of question. Change protected values and sensitive clinical/legal factual combinations, rather than only demographics or names. For symptoms/events/procedures/relationships, select meaningful same-role alternatives, not synonyms of the original. Each peer should differ from the authentic request and from the other peers in its protected factual profile. Repeated mentions must stay consistent within each peer. Change dependent facts together so each peer is independently realistic. Do not force nonsensical substitutions merely for lexical difference; if an abstract role is indispensable to the task, retain the role while changing its concrete realization.
For medical requests, use distinct but plausible patient profiles addressing the same kind of clinical decision, with coherent symptom chronology, tests and medications. For legal requests, use different plausible parties, actions, circumstances and timelines addressing the same kind of legal question. Do not expose an answer, label a request as fictional, append commentary, use placeholders, or make all peers more polished than the original. Return JSON with peers: an array of exactly three full standalone request strings.'''
